  "query_preview": "..A 500 mb longwave trough is slowly moving eastward\nacross northern Arizona. This system is pulling dry air into the\nregion. Satellite measurements of the atmospheric moisture (PW),\nshow values between 0.35-0.45 inches over the High Country, with\n0.45-0.75 inches within the Little Colorado River Valley and the\nlower deserts south of the Mogollon Rim. For comparison, PW was \nsignificantly higher Thursday afternoon, with 0.75 to 1.25 inches \nobserved across the area. \n\nThis dry air, combined with a weak mid-level inversion around \n560mb, will suppress thunderstorm activity this afternoon. This \nwill leave almost all of northern Arizona precipitation free, \nexcept along the eastern Mogollon Rim and White Mountains (Heber- \nOvergaard eastward), where the forecast calls for a 10-20% chance \nof showers and thunderstorms there. \n\nOn Sunday, drier and more stable conditions will suppress shower\nand thunderstorm activity area-wide. A few pockets of freezing \ntemperatures may develop in sheltered valleys above 6500 feet. \n\nOn Monday, an approaching West Coast low begins to influence\nnorthern Arizona weather. Dry and stable conditions continue, but\nmodel guidance suggests a return of isolated showers and perhaps \na thunderstorm or two are possible northwest of Flagstaff. The \ncurrent forecast calls for a 10-20% chance of showers there. \n\nBy Tuesday, the low moves far enough inland to transport moisture\ninto the area for an uptick in shower and thunderstorm activity \nacross much of northern Arizona. At this point, the main focus \nareas for storms are over eastern Arizona and Grand Canyon \nCountry. In addition, southerly wind speeds are forecast to \nincrease, likely remaining below Wind Advisory level thresholds, \nwith gusts near 30 mph forecast over higher terrain. \n\nOn Wednesday, model guidance is in agreement that the low will \nexit the area, but the development of another West Coast trough \ncombined with surges of tropical moisture keeps chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms in the forecast for the remainder of the\nweek.",
